  <section class="roi-method-section" aria-labelledby="roi-method-title">
    <div class="roi-wrap">
      <div class="roi-section-heading"><div><p class="roi-eyebrow">Open arithmetic</p><h2 id="roi-method-title">How we calculate<br>your return</h2></div><p>First-year gross profit from modelled customers, less your full campaign investment. Every input stays under your control.</p></div>
      <details class="roi-method-details">
        <summary>See the formulas, worked example and timing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="roi-method-grid">
          <div class="roi-worked-example"><p class="roi-eyebrow">The starting example</p><h3>Two customers.<br>One business case.</h3><ol><li><span>Campaign investment</span><strong>US$4,000 × 3 months = US$12,000</strong></li><li><span>Modelled new customers</span><strong>20 held meetings × 10% = 2</strong></li><li><span>Potential first-year revenue</span><strong>2 × US$25,000 = US$50,000</strong></li><li><span>Gross profit</span><strong>US$50,000 × 50% = US$25,000</strong></li><li><span>Contribution after campaign cost</span><strong>US$25,000 − US$12,000 = US$13,000</strong></li><li><span>Return on campaign investment</span><strong>US$13,000 ÷ US$12,000 × 100 ≈ 108.3%</strong></li></ol><p>One customer contributes US$12,500 before campaign cost, covering the US$12,000 investment at these assumptions.</p></div>
          <div class="roi-method-notes"><article><span>01 / THE FORMULAS</span><h3>From meetings to contribution.</h3><p><strong>Investment</strong> = monthly cost × months + additional costs. <strong>Customers</strong> = held meetings × close rate. <strong>Revenue</strong> = customers × first-year customer value.</p><p><strong>Gross profit</strong> = revenue × margin. <strong>Contribution</strong> = gross profit − investment. <strong>ROI</strong> = contribution ÷ investment × 100. Convert percentages to decimals when multiplying.</p></article><article><span>02 / BREAK-EVEN</span><h3>The whole-customer target.</h3><p>Divide investment by the gross profit from one customer and round up. A zero customer value or margin cannot produce a finite target; zero investment makes percentage ROI undefined.</p></article><article><span>03 / TIMING</span><h3>Follow the customer beyond the campaign.</h3><p>Outreach can create customers who sign later. The model counts their first-year revenue, which may arrive after the campaign ends. Payment timing, general overhead and tax sit outside this calculation.</p></article></div>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="roi-faq-section roi-screen-only" aria-labelledby="roi-faq-title">
    <div class="roi-wrap roi-faq-grid"><div><p class="roi-eyebrow">Before you commit</p><h2 id="roi-faq-title">Good questions.<br>Clear answers.</h2><p>Understand the assumptions before turning the estimate into a campaign.</p></div><div class="roi-faq-list">
      <details><summary>Can I compare an agency campaign with an in-house sales team?</summary><p>Yes. Enter the full monthly cost for either approach, including the people, management and tools involved. Add recruiting, setup or other one-time costs in Advanced settings. Use the same meeting standard and customer economics for a useful comparison.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does US$4,000 buy the 20 meetings in the example?</summary><p>The two figures are separate inputs. US$4,000 is CallTeam's starting monthly program price; 20 held meetings across three months is an illustrative scenario. Actual scope and expected meeting volume are discussed after reviewing your offer, market, data and qualification standard.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Should I enter meetings per month or for the whole campaign?</summary><p>Enter held meetings across the entire campaign. For three months, add all three months together. Count qualified conversations that take place and exclude cancellations and no-shows.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What close rate should I use?</summary><p>Use your team's conversion from comparable qualified outbound meetings to customers. Match the definition and follow-up period to your own sales process. If you do not have reliable history, compare several assumptions and discuss a realistic starting range with CallTeam.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Should customer value mean lifetime value or annual revenue?</summary><p>Use the customer's first 12 months of revenue, including projects delivered within that period. This keeps the comparison consistent without assuming years of renewals or retention.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Why does gross margin change the result?</summary><p>Every customer also costs money to serve. Gross margin accounts for product or service delivery before the model subtracts campaign investment. Use the margin that reflects your business.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Why are customers sometimes shown as a decimal?</summary><p>The forecast is an average implied by your meeting count and close rate. Real customers arrive in whole numbers. The break-even target rounds up to show how many whole customers would cover the investment.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What happens after I book a time with CallTeam?</summary><p>We review your offer, market, current sales process and calculator scenario. Then we discuss the qualification standard, campaign scope and whether the starting program fits your business.</p></details>
    </div></div>
